<time datetime="2023-03-20">March 20, 2023</time>
<article id="PSDrives" class="WebBits">
    <h2>TRY: Surface PowerShell Drives</h2>
    <p>
        Windows uses PowerShell as an
        <abbr title="Application Programming Interface">API</abbr> to interact
        with the <abbr title="Local Operating System">local OS</abbr> in many
        ways. The scripting language is common for administration automation
        and accessing <abbr title="Client-Side Object Model">CSOM</abbr> and
        <abbr title="Server-Side Object Model">SSOM</abbr> methods.
    </p>
    <p>
        Certificates' drive can be accessed in PowerShell's
        <code>cert:\</code> location. Instead of browsing a drive letter, the
        shell reads information from the isolated os partitions.
    </p>
    <aside class="examplePink">
        <p>PowerShell drives available to review:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>cert:\</li>
            <li>hklm:\</li>
            <li>env:\</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Reading the contents of the drive is similar to changing directories
            <strong>Ex: </strong><code>cd c:\</code> in command prompt, using
            drive <strong>Ex: </strong><code>cd env:\</code>
        </p>
    </aside>
    <figure id="CheckCertificateExpiration">
        <h3 class="Title">
            Check for expiring certificates &lpar;1 year&rpar;
        </h3>
        <pre id="Measure-NetworkSpeed" class="prettyprint linenums">
cd cert:\
dir
cd Cert:\LocalMachine
$today = Get-Date
$OneYearFromToday = $today.AddYears(1)
Get-ChildItem -Recurse | Where-Object{($_.NotAfter -gt $today) `
-and ($_.NotAfter -lt $OneYearFromToday)} `
| fl | Sort-Object NotAfter


</pre>
        <figcaption>
            Paste the above code into PowerShell to test local computer
            certificate expirations.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
        Web administrators are familiar with maintenance needed for
        <abbr title="HyperText Transport Protocol Secure">HTTPS</abbr>
        delivery. Often, certificates will expire on a web or application
        server. The above script would be used to find certificates expiring
        within a 1 year's range.
    </p>
</article>
